1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,066
He we are just going to load the autosave file as an example, it was saved when making the FTP demo video...

2
00:00:02,066 --> 00:00:03,816
Here it will just take a few seconds to load... The renderer loads every 250 packets via the preprocessor in chunks...

3
00:00:03,816 --> 00:00:07,250
So the user can start interacting with packets before the preprocessor finishes.

4
00:00:07,249 --> 00:00:09,400
Here we would initially load into list view, but its going to jump to Stats view becuase that is where the autosave was left off.

5
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:11,733
Here I'm going to load all FTP management packets by clicking it under the Stats tab, where protocols in the capture will appear.

6
00:00:11,783 --> 00:00:16,783
We can review the packet data here on the Host Data tab, but from here I'm going to jump back to the List tab...

7
00:00:16,783 --> 00:00:21,633
This is the List tab, showing all packets in order as they appear in the capture by default, but the columns are, of course, sortable, by clicking the heading.

8
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:25,066
From here you can also export any of this under the mouse "in context" to the Notes tab.

9
00:00:25,066 --> 00:00:30,700
But instead I'm going to load up another packet from the FTP management stream, so I can show you what it looks like...

10
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:36,050
in the Conv tab.  We're loading this entire stream, so multiple packets will be loaded into the conversion workspace, back to back.

11
00:00:36,383 --> 00:00:41,733
But you can also load a packet, payload, or the payload from a decoded protocol, such as HTTP into the Conv tab, using the Context Menu, as well.

12
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:49,150
This what a stream looks like in the Conv tab, or conversions workspace.  As you can see you can also has the payload, or decode the currently loaded protocol.

13
00:00:49,566 --> 00:00:54,566
From here, it looks like it may have captured some credentials, if we got lucky, so lets hop onto the Keystore tab, and check out if it aquired anything...

14
00:00:55,533 --> 00:01:00,533
Oh wow, looks like it captured two usernames and their associated passwords from the FTP management stream! Lets take a peek..

15
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:12,483
Looks like it grabbed a password for the user "user1", and a password for "anonymous" when they logged in, thats cool.

16
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:19,933
Lets highlight that password to put it into context, so we can easily put it in the Notes tab, then we can refer to it later on.

17
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:38,783
Here's the notes tab, pretty basic, you can colorcode your notes too.

18
00:01:38,783 --> 00:01:41,350
Lets get back over to the List tab, so we can view the FTP-DATA stream, which is where our file is.

19
00:01:41,366 --> 00:01:49,816
We can use PacketSnitch's file carving features to rip whatever file the user transferred out of the FTP-DATA stream, so here goes....

20
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:03,116
If you need to, just check the log to see where it saved the data...

21
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:08,266
Finally, lets save our session as something memorable so we can load it again!

22
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:10,199
Hope you had fun following along in this PacketSnitch demo!!!

